WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.750
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.380
My name is Sten Vermund.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.530
I'm proud to serve as the dean

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.920
of the Yale School of Public Health,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.320
and I'm delighted to welcome you

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.000
to the Global Health conversation series,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.400
sponsored by the Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.490
This series is a venue for
our community to learn from

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.850
and connect with innovative
leaders in global health.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.300
In addition to YIGH,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.860
we're indebted to the George
Herbert Walker, Jr. lecture

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.210
in International Studies

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.330
at the Whitney and Betty MacMillan

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.630
Center for International
and Area Studies at Yale,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:44.000
for sponsoring this series,
including today's event.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.940
So it's a pleasure to be with you today

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.260
and to introduce our speaker.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.260
Each year since 1988, on
December 2nd yesterday,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.510
we've commemorated World AIDS Day.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.180
I remember vividly the very first one.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.180
I had started my job
at the NIH on that day.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.510
And this highlighted the urgent need

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.820
and the inequalities
that drive HIV and AIDS

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.120
and other pandemics around the world.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.150
The theme for this year's World AIDS Day,

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:22.150
is end inequalities,
end AIDS, end pandemics.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.440
So 40 years plus, since the
first AIDS cases were reported,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.530
HIV still threatens the world.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.290
Today, the world is off track

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.720
from delivering on the shared commitment

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.980
to end AIDS by 2030,

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.900
not because of a lack of
knowledge or tools to beat AIDS,

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.670
but because of structural inequalities

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.570
that obstruct proven solutions

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.383
to HIV prevention and treatment.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.940
As well as being central to ending AIDS,

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.310
tackling inequalities

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.380
will advance the human
rights of key populations,

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.020
and people who are living with HIV,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.360
make societies better
prepared to beat COVID-19

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.840
and other pandemics

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.763
and support economic
recovery and stability.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.240
Fulfilling the promise
to tackle inequalities

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.300
will save millions of lives,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.823
and will benefit society
in myriad of ways.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.230
To celebrate or
commemorate World AIDS Day,

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.730
and discuss some of
these important issues,

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.480
we are so pleased to have with us today,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.480
Dr. Angeli Achrekar, the acting
US Global AIDS Coordinator

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.870
and special representative

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.040
for global health diplomacy
in the Department of State.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.840
Dr. Achrekar has dedicated her career

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.080
as a public servant to global HIV AIDS,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.000
women and girls health and
global public health development.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.320
After working in India

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.650
and later with UNICEF
headquarters on adolescent health,

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:59.650
she accepted position with US CDC in 2001,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.810
where she focused

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.880
on integrated adolescent
health's programs and services.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.590
Since 2003, Dr. Achrekar

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:11.500
has been working on PEPFAR's
global HIV AIDS efforts.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.810
And in 2005, she went to South Africa

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.120
to help local governments

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.860
plan and implement a rapid
assessment of drug use,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.300
and sexual HIV risk patterns,

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.100
among injecting and non-injecting
drug users and sex workers

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.610
in three cities Durban,
Pretoria and Cape Town.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.550
After serving as country officer for CDC,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.950
she became the senior
public health manager

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.050
supporting cross cutting
system wide sustainability

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.520
activities and strategies

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.060
in CDC's division of global HIV AIDS.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.840
In 2011, she joined the
US Department of State

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.220
and helped lead the conceptualization

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.920
and development of saving
mothers giving life,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.040
as well as facilitating

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.770
the implementation of
this important program.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.330
For those of you who don't know about it,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.340
it was a public and private partnership

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.110
that impacted maternal and neonatal health

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.773
in the context of HIV service delivery.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.860
Dr. Achrekar holds a
doctorate in public health,

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.490
from a very good program,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.280
the University of North
Carolina Chapel Hill.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.170
We love that program, we
love our friends at UNC,

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:25.770
but most importantly,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.160
she has her Master's of Public Health

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.230
from the Yale School of Public Health.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:34.503
So welcome Dr. Achrekar
back to your alma mater.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.060
- Thank you so much, Dr. Vermund,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.913
and to the entire Yale community,

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.360
and the Yale University but
specifically a huge thank you

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.140
to the Yale Institute of Global Health.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.320
I'm just so privileged to be here with you

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.780
commemorating World AIDS
Day that occurred yesterday.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:01.020
I have a few slides to cover
and to share the experience,

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.890
and to provide an update
on where we are globally,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:05.800
if that's okay.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.220
So if I could start with the presentation

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.810
to really walk you through
commemorating World AIDS Day,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:15.330
and talking specifically

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.290
about the impact that
we've had as a globe,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.580
through the President's
Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:22.933
On the next slide, please.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.630
I always start every presentation

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:32.780
by talking about how
critical and instrumental

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.840
the unwavering bipartisan congressional

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.410
and presidential leadership
of PEPFAR has been.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:44.040
We are 18 years in this
fight through PEPFAR,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.040
which again started with
President George W. Bush in 2003.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:52.960
Each subsequent president
has picked up the mantle

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.950
and really led and supported this program

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.560
with an incredible amount of effort.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.290
We've also enjoyed bipartisan
support across 10 congresses

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:10.080
over the nearly two decades
of the program's life.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.470
Most recently with the
current administration,

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.100
the Biden Harris administration,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.970
I can't even begin to tell you

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.420
how incredible their support has been.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.630
And I'm pleased to be
able to talk with you

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.490
in the next couple of slides here,

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:28.490
about how President
Biden really leaned in,

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.290
particularly on this recent
World AIDS Day, yesterday,

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.830
but really how the administration
has been leaning in

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.930
and building upon

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.763
the successes of all the
previous administrations since.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.683
So on the next slide.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:51.400
Yesterday, President Biden
hosted an incredible event

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.780
at the White House,

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.740
where the domestic and
global HIV community

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.260
was brought together,

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:02.900
private sector, universities, Congress,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.820
faith-based organizations, activists,

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.250
it was an extraordinary
in the US government,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:12.620
it was an extraordinary
coalition of people,

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:16.700
of passion that has been
built over the past 40 years

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:17.653
in this fight.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.010
As a part of his remarks,

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.650
he announced PEPFAR's
latest global results,

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.083
which are nothing short of extraordinary.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.600
He announced that together through PEPFAR,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.040
with countries and communities,

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.710
we've saved more than 20 million lives,

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.710
we've prevented millions
of HIV infections,

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.810
and helped at least 20 countries

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.790
bring their HIV epidemics under control.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:47.270
This is extraordinary, particularly
in the face of COVID-19.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.050
But just full stop,
without a vaccine or cure

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:54.380
to have nearly 19 million people,

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.300
women, men and children
on life saving treatment,

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.600
for example, is incredible,

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.573
to have prevented nearly 28 million,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:08.120
or to have conducted nearly
28 million circumcisions

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.730
in order to prevent HIV infections
among men and young boys.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.380
Again, nothing short of extraordinary.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:15.383
Next slide, please.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.340
We have over the years,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:24.640
supported population-based
surveillance studies,

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.440
household surveillance studies,

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:31.440
to really validate results
that we're seeing in countries.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.340
Here we're seeing a
somewhat of a busy slide,

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.150
but what's important to note,

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.240
is how far countries have come

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.830
in terms of achieving their 90, 90, 90,

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.690
sustainable development goals.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.370
This is tracking the percentage of PLH,

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:54.500
people living with HIV that
are aware of their status

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.060
and those that are aware of their status

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.640
that are on life saving treatment,

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.560
and then those that are on treatment,

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:01.230
that are virally suppressed.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:05.700
So you can see the bars in
terms of the percent positive,

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.180
the percent on treatment,

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.930
and then those that are virally suppressed

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.000
in red, blue and gray respectively.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.700
What's important also
to note on the slide,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:22.266
is the percentage in orange,

00:09:22.266 --> 00:09:26.060
which is the community viral suppression.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.250
It's absolutely incredible
when you look particularly

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:30.940
at the left side of this graph here,

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.030
to see Botswana at 93, 98, 98,

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.940
with 89% community viral suppression,

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.030
Malawi, a very resource limited country,

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:46.030
at 88, 98, 97% with 84% viral suppression

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.340
at the community level.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.800
Just extraordinary how
far countries have come

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.800
together with communities

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.800
as a result of Americans
leadership and generosity

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.450
in really working on this fight together.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:04.283
Next slide.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.670
It's important also to note

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.340
that even in the midst of COVID-19,

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:18.340
that PEPFAR has continued
to not only maintain

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:21.470
the number of people that
we have on treatment,

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.290
but we've continued to grow

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.450
the number of people that
we have on treatment,

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.380
despite the peaks in
COVID-19 transmission.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.900
So on this slide across,

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.900
in color, you can see the number
of waves of COVID 19 cases

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.260
per million people

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.410
in a select number of
countries around the globe.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:47.870
And in red, you can see
the number of individuals

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:52.870
that have continued and
grown on life saving ART.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:54.553
Next slide.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.920
I also wanted to show here

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.343
that we have had challenges
as a result of COVID-19.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:09.530
These data show our circumcision
performance over time,

00:11:10.359 --> 00:11:12.850
over a few year period.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:17.480
You can see where COVID in
quarter three of fiscal year 20

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:21.425
really impacted the program,

00:11:21.425 --> 00:11:24.240
where the services dropped dramatically.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:27.400
This was intentional, we had made sure,

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.640
that we were doing
everything that we could,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.010
at the height of COVID-19,

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:35.250
to really protect the
people that we were serving

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:38.350
to keep them out of the way of COVID,

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:42.850
of infection from COVID-19.

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.160
What we are seeing over time, however,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.940
is that with adaptation to the program,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:52.940
we've been able to rebound
and stabilize the program

00:11:53.241 --> 00:11:54.760
over the quarters.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.860
And this is quarter three data,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:01.860
but shows that 70% achievement
toward our annual target,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.610
again, this is complementary
to where we were

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:06.950
in previous fiscal year.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.270
So again, we've been able to rebound

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:13.390
and this is really
because of incredible work

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:15.280
that has happened at the country level

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.860
by extraordinary people and partners

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.120
that we're working with,

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.760
and real time adaptation to the program.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:22.593
Next slide.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:26.230
And here just gives you a sense

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.170
of some of the prevention services

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:31.840
and pre-exposure prophylaxis

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.810
for adolescent girls and young women.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:37.820
Similarly, you can see a dip
in our prevention services

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.920
for adolescent girls and young women

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:43.440
on the left side of the
graph, but a stark increase,

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.810
after we started to
really adapt the program

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:49.120
and use different modalities

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.030
to implement prevention activities.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.420
And with pre-exposure prophylaxis here,

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.070
you see sort of a stabilization,

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:02.550
through COVID-19 when
the impact really came,

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.370
but the important thing here,

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:09.000
is a real steep incline through COVID.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.090
This shows us some of the preferences

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:13.710
that the people we serve have,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:17.020
and so PrEP is something
that we're really actively

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:18.010
trying to scale up

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.840
not just for adolescent
girls and young women,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.550
but all populations.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:22.383
Next slide.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.130
Over the while,

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:31.130
while we have been a
categorical HIV focused program,

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:35.540
and we've been working to
sustain through COVID-19,

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:40.540
sustain those HIV gains, we
have been pivoting and adapting

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.930
to really respond in the wake of COVID-19.

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:48.320
We often talk about the PEPFAR platform.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.540
And what does that mean?

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.260
Well, what we've learned over time,

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:57.100
is that all of the effort
that we've been able to do

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:01.380
in partnership with countries,
communities and partners,

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:06.380
over the years on HIV services
and delivering HIV services,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.790
has effectively created
a care delivery platform

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:15.200
that has, yes been instrumental to HIV,

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:18.600
but has also recently been instrumental

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.630
to responding to COVID-19.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:26.060
We have thousands of US government teams,

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.990
from the various agencies,
CDC, USA, Peace Corps,

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:33.240
the Department of
Defense, all of the very,

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.620
NIH, all the various
health and human services,

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.860
operating divisions and others.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:42.810
We have local, strong
local implementing partners

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.260
and trusted relationships
with partner governments,

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.540
all in 55 countries around the globe.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:53.190
These relationships, these
people, these partners,

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:57.090
together with the
incredible health system,

00:14:57.090 --> 00:15:00.500
infrastructure investments
that we have made

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:03.392
over the course of PEPFAR's life,

00:15:03.392 --> 00:15:08.392
which is really, been
expressed as over $15 billion,

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:14.760
but tangibly resulted in
over 3000 laboratories

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.340
just on the continent of Africa,

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:18.770
that have been established and networked,

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:20.800
including reference laboratories.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:24.040
Over 70,000 healthcare facilities

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:28.420
in which we work with
direct access to clients

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:32.830
as well as granular data
at all of these facilities.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.020
Nearly 300,000 healthcare workers

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:39.353
that we have trained on HIV, doctors,

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.880
nurses, community health workers,

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.650
epidemiologist, laboratorians, etc,

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:48.270
in a robust commodity supply
chain that is in place.

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:51.200
It is this system, this platform,

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:56.200
that has been critical
to respond to COVID-19.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:01.350
Since COVID really hit
the globe in early 2020,

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:07.350
we've been adapting our program
and leveraging this platform

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:10.140
to support COVID-19 screening,

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:13.650
diagnostics, infection
prevention and control,

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:17.400
demand generation for vaccination,

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:21.390
as well as administration
of vaccines themselves.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.390
I think Secretary Blinken said it best,

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:28.390
when he was recently in
Africa a couple of weeks ago,

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.837
where he said, "PEPFAR has
saved millions of lives,

00:16:31.837 --> 00:16:33.157
"brought the world to the edge

00:16:33.157 --> 00:16:34.927
"of the first AIDS free generation

00:16:34.927 --> 00:16:37.867
"and transformed public
health infrastructure,

00:16:37.867 --> 00:16:41.787
"where the investments we made
years ago in labs and clinics

00:16:41.787 --> 00:16:44.800
"formed the backbone of this
nation's COVID response."

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.500
And he was specifically
talking about Nigeria,

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.380
but this is true across the board.

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:51.213
Next slide.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.430
And the President himself,

00:16:55.430 --> 00:17:00.430
yesterday during the World AIDS
Day ceremony, said it again,

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:03.877
with his words,

00:17:03.877 --> 00:17:07.157
"By strengthening countries'
abilities to fight AIDS,

00:17:07.157 --> 00:17:09.657
"we've also improved
our collective ability

00:17:09.657 --> 00:17:11.520
"to fight other diseases."

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:12.920
And we couldn't agree more.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:13.753
Next slide.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:20.000
So as I noted, since early March,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.890
I would say or late February 2020,

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:26.210
we have been adapting the PEPFAR program

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:30.820
and providing technical
guidance on how we can lean in,

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.410
to leverage the PEPFAR
supported health systems

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:34.950
and infrastructure, and as I noted,

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:39.490
to do everything from COVID-19
screening and diagnostics,

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:42.960
and now all the way to vaccination.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:43.793
Next slide.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:48.330
One of the things that
we did at the outset,

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.850
when COVID-19 first hit was,

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:54.433
was really make sure we
can protect the HIV gains.

00:17:55.693 --> 00:17:57.260
We did a number of things

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:01.770
to ensure that we were focused
on person-centered care

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.240
to protect the populations

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:07.160
most at risk for poor COVID-19
infection outcomes as well.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:12.160
So things that we
implemented with urgency was,

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:16.697
making sure that people on treatment had,

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:21.280
we had absolute connection with them,

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.300
tracking methodology platforms in place

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:25.820
so that we can ensure

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:28.970
that their treatment was uninterrupted.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.930
We rolled out multi-month
dispensing in a matter of weeks,

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:33.930
we have been,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:37.580
this is something that we have been at

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.350
with country governments for years,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.170
but it wasn't until COVID-19,

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:43.110
that country governments realized

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.090
the importance to move quickly,

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:51.090
to ensure three and six months
of drugs for stable clients.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:53.870
This was a way to decompress facilities,

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:54.930
and also make sure

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:58.810
that we were reducing the
potential exposure of COVID-19

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.520
to people living with HIV.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:03.480
We decentralized drug delivery,

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:07.350
we went closest to home,
closest to the communities

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.153
where the people we serve are.

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:14.420
We used virtual platforms,
telemedicine and other ways,

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:17.700
to communicate with
beneficiaries of our programs.

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.160
A lot of the prevention programming

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:24.410
that we had traditionally done
in-person, in group settings

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.820
was not possible during COVID-19.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:29.370
And so we were able to adapt

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:32.800
and institute virtual platforms quickly.

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:36.690
And I can't say enough about
empowering communities,

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:40.360
and how important communities
and local partners have been

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:44.570
in this fight but
particularly through COVID-19.

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:47.710
Community led monitoring
to ensure quality services

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:50.620
and leveraging community health workers

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:52.540
has been absolutely critical.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:53.373
Next slide.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:56.530
This is just an example,

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:59.440
wanted to show some data
of the rapid increase

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.410
in the percentage of
people living with HIV,

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:07.410
that were on ART comparing
fiscal year 20 quarter one data,

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:11.000
to fiscal year 21 quarter three data.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:13.930
And these data reflect,

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:17.700
though the multi-month
dispensing that I noted earlier,

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.240
you can see when you look at
the left side of the graph

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:22.600
compared to the right side of the graph,

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:26.220
the increase in three to six months

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:29.560
of drugs for stable clients.

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:31.300
So again, country by country,

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:35.163
you can see a real increase in shift from,

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.650
toward, I should say,
more months of drugs.

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:46.730
And this has been a saving
grace in the wake of COVID-19.

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:47.563
Next slide.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:54.560
We've been so grateful that
the Congress and this President

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:57.930
appropriated additional resources

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:02.250
specifically for us to
respond to COVID-19.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.970
We received $250 million

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:08.870
to prevent and mitigate COVID-19 impacts

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:11.300
on PEPFAR programs and beneficiaries.

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:13.040
And I guess I should start by saying,

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:14.830
I didn't say this earlier,

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:19.830
PEPFAR is a tremendous effort
implemented in 55 countries,

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:23.470
but over the past 18 years,

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:28.470
reflects $100 billion of
American taxpayer dollars

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:34.023
in this enormous global HIV response.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:39.830
But that was focused on HIV specifically,

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:41.660
receiving $250 million

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:46.650
to really focus in on utilizing the system

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.250
and the platform we have in place

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:52.250
to really respond to
COVID-19 has been critical.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:54.650
So we were pleased

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:57.780
to be able to program
these resources quickly,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:02.150
so that we could continue
to utilize the platform

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:05.070
to help respond specifically to COVID-19.

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:05.903
Next slide.

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:10.170
I wanted to provide a
couple of country examples,

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:13.550
and these are hot off the
press as of 7 a.m this morning,

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:18.330
when we had a global call home
with all of the 55 countries.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:22.310
To really talk about how
we can and should continue

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:27.310
to lean in and utilize the
PEPFAR supported platform

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:32.710
to help in COVID
vaccination in particular,

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:36.120
particularly with the recent variant

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.900
and the need for us to
really mobilize quickly.

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.680
This is an example from South Africa,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.700
with their ongoing COVID-19
vaccination support.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:46.930
And these are just examples

00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:51.500
of ways that we are bringing
both of these efforts together,

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:54.840
the HIV response and the COVID-19 response

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:59.840
to be nimble and to be impactful

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:03.870
as COVID-19 continues to
rage on the continent.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.570
So we're doing everything here

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:08.410
from supporting the healthcare workforce,

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:10.970
that is doing everything
from data capturing

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:14.340
to vaccination in arms, as I said earlier,

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:18.070
to procuring equipment
and protective equipment,

00:23:18.070 --> 00:23:20.410
vaccine consumables, freezers,

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:23.640
ways to support vaccine delivery,

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:26.140
transport, logistic support.

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:28.970
Data systems are absolutely critical,

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:33.970
including supporting the
national department of health

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:37.780
and working together.

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:39.610
So again, these are ways

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:42.370
that we are bringing
these efforts together.

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:43.313
On the next slide.

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.940
And here's an example from Zambia,

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:51.510
really important and critical work,

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:53.870
that they've been able to do,

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:58.870
focusing in on PEPFAR
supported facilities where,

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:04.230
that are focused for
delivering antiretrovirals

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:06.400
to people living with HIV.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:08.590
So a real intentional focus

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:11.170
on vaccinating people living with HIV

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:13.570
in existing ART clinics.

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:18.290
And so what they have seen
in a matter of weeks really,

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:21.720
is a focused effort in these ART clinics,

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:25.500
in high burden areas in Lusaka.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:28.300
In a week we're able to
vaccinate 1000 people

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:31.080
living with HIV, again,

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:35.830
and that mushroomed where other facilities

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:39.900
across now over 500 facilities,

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:42.580
across all 10 provinces in Zambia

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:46.063
are rolling out this program.

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:50.740
Really pushing COVID-19 vaccination

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:52.220
for people living with HIV

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:55.640
as well as family members and
others that accompany them.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:59.880
The ministry of health was
so thrilled about this,

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.360
that they issued a circular,

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.990
which is, there's a picture
here in late August,

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:09.990
they issued a circular expanding
this policy and this effort

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.010
across all 10 provinces.

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:14.288
So we're really excited about this,

00:25:14.288 --> 00:25:18.310
and looking forward to what
might be able to be achieved

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:19.820
by bringing these efforts together.

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:20.653
Next slide.

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:24.270
And again, they used the opportunity

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:26.160
of World AIDS Day yesterday,

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:30.890
to really bring together a
COVID-19 vaccination surge

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:33.070
with World AIDS Day activities.

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:37.367
So bringing a really high level push

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:40.980
from the government of Zambia,

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.000
together with the US government

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:44.550
to see what might be possible

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:46.137
by the end of this fiscal year,

00:25:46.137 --> 00:25:48.600
oh sorry, by the end
of this calendar year,

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:53.570
by bringing a real push
for COVID-19 vaccination.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:54.403
Next slide.

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:59.050
There are of course
challenges and opportunities

00:25:59.050 --> 00:26:01.310
to sustaining epidemic control of HIV.

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:04.930
And as I noted, and showed in
the population based surveys,

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:06.830
there are countries that are getting,

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:09.140
that new infections are
continuing to drive down

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:13.680
and all cause mortality is
being driven down as well.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:17.130
Countries are beginning to
control their epidemics.

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:18.310
But there are challenges,

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:21.470
including inequities
that continue to persist

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:23.630
among adolescent girls and young women,

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:25.300
key populations and children.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:28.720
And these are areas where we
are trying to double down.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:31.160
COVID-19, of course continues to persist,

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:32.150
and this is something

00:26:32.150 --> 00:26:36.480
that we need to continue to
proactively get ahead of,

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:41.360
to ensure that we're not just
protecting the gains in HIV,

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.470
but continuing to advance
and accelerate those gains

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:46.890
in the midst of COVID-19.

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:48.860
And there are real opportunities

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:50.756
to tackle some of these inequities

00:26:50.756 --> 00:26:53.920
by working differently across sectors,

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.620
by really bringing a refreshed perspective

00:26:56.620 --> 00:27:00.770
to how we're disaggregating data

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:05.430
across younger and more specific
populations, for example,

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:07.810
to drive where our investments go.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:12.200
And then again, utilizing the
platform in different ways

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:13.970
to help respond for the future.

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:14.803
Next slide.

00:27:16.730 --> 00:27:19.023
There are some elements about,

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:26.640
what a sustained response
to epidemic control of HIV

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:27.473
might look like.

00:27:27.473 --> 00:27:30.560
And it really has to do with
equitable health services

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:34.030
and solutions across the board.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.590
But there are elements

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:39.120
that include a whole of domestic approach,

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:40.250
between the government's,

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:42.320
the partner governments and communities,

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:44.950
enabling policy and
programmatic environments

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:49.260
that remain barriers to services,

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.110
but that having these in
place are absolutely critical.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:54.110
Sufficient functional capacity

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:57.660
to maintain these programs
and these services,

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:02.140
financial sustainability
is essential as well,

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:04.800
domestic resource mobilization.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:07.330
And then integrating HIV services

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:10.920
into the broader public and
private care delivery systems,

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:12.410
to really ensure

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:17.410
that when we have a country
at epidemic control of HIV,

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:21.970
that we are really
ensuring that that control,

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:26.970
which is a very dynamic
place remains in control.

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:30.530
And so ensuring that a
robust public health response

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:34.060
to monitor and track
existing and emerging threats

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:37.470
becomes even more critical,
as does quality assurance.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:38.303
Next slide.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:45.160
Just a couple of slides on finances.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:46.940
HIV investments by Funder.

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:51.450
This slide shows by country on
the right side of the graph,

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:53.870
the percent contribution from PEPFAR,

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:56.770
the US government in
blue, the Global Fund,

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:00.560
which the US is a big contributor to,

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:02.500
and then partner governments in gray.

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:05.030
And we continue to work seamlessly

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:06.590
across partner governments,

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:08.920
the Global Fund and with the US government

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:12.360
to make sure that our
investments and services

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:14.160
complement one another.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:14.993
Next slide.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:21.870
But we continue to be
concerned because of COVID-19.

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:25.440
When we look at the projected
gross domestic product growth

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.870
versus HIV prevalence rate
as seen on this graph,

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:33.190
you can see economic problems
from COVID across the board,

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.170
but are pervasive and worse

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:37.440
in countries with high HIV prevalence

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.580
as noted in the red dots

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.580
on the right side of the graph here.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:42.413
Next slide.

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.797
One approach that we've been utilizing,

00:29:47.797 --> 00:29:52.797
is sustaining delivery of HIV services

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:55.170
through local and community partners.

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:58.180
This has been an approach
to sustainability

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:00.900
that we have been trying to employ

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:02.840
for a number of years now.

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:05.590
And we're seeing significant gains

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:08.220
and movement in the right direction here,

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:10.270
where we're really working to make sure

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:13.230
that funding is going directly

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:15.670
to local and community
partners themselves,

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:18.330
as you can see in the
graph across the country.

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:23.330
Some countries have over
80% of their funding

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:25.850
going to local partners.

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:27.250
And it's not just the funding,

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:29.410
it's ensuring that the local capacity

00:30:29.410 --> 00:30:32.530
and capabilities of these partners exist

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:37.530
to implement services in
the respective countries.

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:38.603
Next slide, please.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:43.300
Just a couple of final slides here,

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:46.990
just to note that we're
really bringing an equity lens

00:30:46.990 --> 00:30:51.220
to the effort to complement
our work on accountability,

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:52.730
transparency and impact,

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:54.910
to really make sure
we're closing those gaps

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:56.140
that I noted earlier.

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:57.240
And on the next slide.

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:01.100
And I'll stop here, which is,

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:03.010
we've been working with many of you

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:05.539
and others across the globe,

00:31:05.539 --> 00:31:09.250
to develop a PEPFAR strategy
for the next five years,

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.840
to help guide our efforts moving forward.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:14.830
And these really relate to
accomplishing the mission,

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:18.420
finishing what we started,
building enduring capabilities,

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:21.630
as I noted earlier, how
critical health systems,

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:23.850
enabling environments
and local partners are,

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:26.980
and finally building
lasting collaborations.

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:27.813
Next slide.

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:31.190
And with that, I'll just say thank you,

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:34.063
and turn it back over to you Saad.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:38.350
- Hi.

00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:43.350
So Dean Vermund was,
started the event on our,

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:47.930
hosted event on behalf of Yale
Institute for Global Health,

00:31:47.930 --> 00:31:52.420
unfortunately, he is in
transit in Dominican Republic.

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:54.470
So while he is connected,

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:56.480
I will be filling in for him

00:31:56.480 --> 00:31:58.193
for the question answer sessions.

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:03.150
And, we'd be happy to
sort of fill questions

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:06.420
from the audience, as well
as, a few other questions,

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:07.990
that we've been thinking about.

00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:11.750
So first of all, thank
you for a really nice talk

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:16.750
and a comprehensive overview
of the progress of PEPFAR.

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:20.190
And what are some of the things

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:21.733
PEPFAR has been doing lately.

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:24.960
And those of you who don't
know me, I'm Saad Omer,

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.200
I'm the director of Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:29.970
So, the first question

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:34.970
actually came from our
participants, our audience.

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:38.060
And I think it's very timely

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:41.750
in the context of the overall environment.

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:46.200
Because there was very strong
bipartisan support for PEPFAR,

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:49.730
when it was first launched.

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:51.273
Do you still see that support?

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:54.720
- Thanks so much Saad.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:58.060
I have to say, you're right,

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:00.070
when PEPFAR was first launched,

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:02.963
there was incredible bipartisan support,

00:33:04.471 --> 00:33:08.960
across the US government with
President George W. Bush,

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:10.740
who was extraordinary

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:14.060
in launching such an incredible effort,

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:18.320
but also across the Congress,
from Republicans to Democrats.

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:21.730
You know, there's something
magnificent about the HIV fight,

00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:25.400
it brings all walks of life together.

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:28.640
And what we've seen over the
years in the past 18 years,

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:33.270
and what I noted in my
presentation, is that,

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:36.800
every year since, every
administration since

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:39.260
and every change of Congress since,

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:43.330
they have equally come in and brought,

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:45.730
stood on the other's shoulders,

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:48.550
and really continued to bring

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:51.470
the highest level of congressional

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:54.040
and presidential bipartisan leadership.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:58.993
And as I noted, most recently
with President Biden,

00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:03.130
it wasn't just with World AIDS Day,

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:07.800
where the enormous support and leadership

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:11.410
of the global response was displayed.

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:14.760
Since day one of this administration,

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:18.910
President Biden and the administration

00:34:18.910 --> 00:34:20.680
has really put their arm,

00:34:20.680 --> 00:34:25.543
have put their arms around this
issue supporting all of us,

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:28.590
and the effort and the fight,

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:32.040
in every way that we have come together

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:34.480
in the multilateral space.

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:37.650
There was the UN high
level meeting this summer,

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:39.070
that happens every five years,

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:42.320
where the administration
showed full support,

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:45.230
but all the way to the
way the budget looks,

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:49.404
to the way, again, the
political leadership as well.

00:34:49.404 --> 00:34:54.404
So they've also recently
announced, that the US will host

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:58.130
the next replenishment of the Global Fund,

00:34:58.130 --> 00:35:00.500
to really make sure that we are advancing

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:05.230
our efforts for HIV, TB, and
malaria around the globe,

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:07.830
through our multilateral efforts as well.

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:10.860
So the bipartisan support
has been critical,

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:14.810
it's been maintained and
grown over the years,

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:18.730
and we're incredibly grateful for that.

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:20.260
- That's wonderful.

00:35:20.260 --> 00:35:23.943
So the second question comes
actually from Dean Vermund.

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:28.350
And he raised an interesting point,

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:31.140
that it's obviously really good to see

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:36.140
that PEPFAR has aided in
the COVID-19 response,

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:39.920
especially the in-country infrastructure,

00:35:39.920 --> 00:35:42.340
has been repurposed, utilized,

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:43.770
and by the way not for the first time

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:46.420
for a major public health emergency.

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:50.460
Can you comment on other
expansion of mission

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:53.800
as with the non communicable diseases,

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:57.050
and people have sort of
strong opinions on that,

00:35:57.050 --> 00:36:00.750
but one is that, it's a no brainer,

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:03.010
that you have built that infrastructure,

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:04.500
and there's this new,

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:09.100
not new, but this emerging
and important issue

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:10.850
of non communicable disease.

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:12.190
But on the other hand,

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:14.630
there's this instinct a few people have,

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.920
is to preserve the capacity

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:19.312
for a purpose that it was created.

00:36:19.312 --> 00:36:24.083
So we'd love to hear your
thoughts about expansion to NCDs.

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:27.530
- Yeah, no, thank you for
that important question.

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.940
And, as we as,

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:33.590
you're right, in that the PEPFAR platform

00:36:33.590 --> 00:36:37.290
hasn't only been utilized for COVID-19,

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:39.730
there have been so many
ways over the years,

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:44.730
whether it's other
infectious, areas like Ebola,

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:50.010
or when H1N1, SARS, MERS,
so many other areas,

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:55.440
but we've also been utilizing
the platform to respond to TB,

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:02.203
to respond to cervical cancer.

00:37:03.041 --> 00:37:06.630
And so those are examples
of how we've expanded

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.110
to help really move

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:16.110
in the direction of non
communicable diseases.

00:37:16.570 --> 00:37:19.620
I think the question is a really good one.

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:24.620
Because there is a very delicate
dance that we have to do,

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:27.280
in order to make sure

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:32.280
that we don't dilute our efforts in HIV.

00:37:35.630 --> 00:37:40.530
While we do what we know
is the smart thing to do,

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:45.530
which is to utilize capacity
that exists to help respond.

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:50.667
And so that's why we've been
very deliberate and careful

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:56.720
about how we've been responding
to COVID-19, for example,

00:37:57.470 --> 00:38:01.720
tying it to looking at
the data and making sure

00:38:01.720 --> 00:38:06.720
that we are doing everything
to preserve the HIV gains,

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:11.230
while we've been able to
expand as we've needed to.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:14.650
I think we would be,

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:17.080
look, we haven't finished the fight yet.

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:22.080
And that's why I mentioned,
there are gaps that persist,

00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:25.430
and we've gotta close those gaps for HIV.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:26.310
But we've gotta,

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:29.910
we can't also ignore the fact that,

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:33.050
there is this incredible
system that exists,

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:34.960
and whether we like it or not,

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:39.880
countries realize and see
the power of the systems

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:41.910
and capabilities that are in place,

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:46.910
and see the need to be able
to bring synergy together.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:52.120
So my thought is, we
need all ships to rise,

00:38:52.120 --> 00:38:54.960
we need to utilize that
platform that exists,

00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:58.690
because starting from
scratch doesn't make sense.

00:38:58.690 --> 00:38:59.923
But we've gotta be able to do it

00:38:59.923 --> 00:39:02.020
in a very careful and considerate way,

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:05.150
so that the outcomes

00:39:05.150 --> 00:39:09.940
that we've been able to
achieve together on HIV,

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:11.297
don't backslide.

00:39:13.878 --> 00:39:16.532
- Yeah, and we got a sort
of a related question,

00:39:16.532 --> 00:39:17.839
saying that, and obviously,

00:39:17.839 --> 00:39:20.110
we definitely don't want
to lose the progress

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:22.600
that we have all made so far,

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:26.710
and diversify the HIV AIDS funding sources

00:39:26.710 --> 00:39:29.610
to address the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:32.880
So, as someone who sort
of has run this kind of,

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:34.970
a program of this magnitude,

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:39.033
do you think there's a need
for a new fund or some sort of,

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:45.702
another entity or a resource for COVID-19

00:39:45.702 --> 00:39:47.100
and future pandemics.

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:52.100
Because we don't have to
have a pie of a fixed size,

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:57.560
we can increase the
size of the pie itself,

00:39:57.560 --> 00:40:00.760
public health is extremely cost efficient,

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:03.340
we get really high returns on investment.

00:40:03.340 --> 00:40:04.940
But would you think there is a value

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:07.233
on a separate fund for
pandemics or COVID-19,

00:40:08.277 --> 00:40:09.923
or something of that sort?

00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:15.220
- So, the global health architecture

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:16.790
and what the future looks like,

00:40:16.790 --> 00:40:19.780
is a hot topic of conversation,

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:23.203
as I'm sure you're very well
aware of and immersed in.

00:40:24.650 --> 00:40:28.100
And, I think,

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:30.630
and part of the reason I showed

00:40:30.630 --> 00:40:33.250
what was possible with $250 million

00:40:34.360 --> 00:40:36.950
of the American Rescue Plan funds,

00:40:36.950 --> 00:40:41.950
was that with a relatively
small amount of money,

00:40:42.707 --> 00:40:47.707
$250 million in our $6
billion a year program,

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:50.610
how much we've been able to do,

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:54.140
because we have systems and capacity

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:56.240
and infrastructure in place.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:41:00.727
So, I guess, setting up a fund,

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:09.840
specifically for pandemic
preparedness and response,

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:12.270
there's certainly a different skills,

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:15.170
there's a broader skill set
that needs to be brought to bear

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:20.170
that is not solely in the
HIV sphere, but the time,

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:24.043
the time that might be involved
to do something like that,

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.560
when we should be,

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:29.890
when we're in the midst of
two pandemics right now,

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:34.103
HIV and COVID-19, and being
able to respond quickly,

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:39.140
that's only possible with infrastructure

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:42.270
that is on the ground and
capacity on the ground right now

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:43.410
to mobilize quickly.

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:47.050
And so, I guess that's
the long way of saying,

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:48.090
I think a fund,

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:52.080
I think a separate fund,

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:53.890
again, we'll see where the conversation

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.800
of global health architecture falls,

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:59.310
but there's definitely
something to be said,

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:04.040
for building on investments
that have already been made,

00:42:04.040 --> 00:42:09.040
because that's a fast
efficient way to work.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:12.353
- Yeah, though I think,

00:42:12.353 --> 00:42:14.560
and, of course, we'll have to think about,

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:19.130
the horizontal versus
vertical nature of programs.

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:22.730
You know, obviously, there
are trade offs there,

00:42:22.730 --> 00:42:26.380
on both sides, or sort
of the diagonal nature

00:42:26.380 --> 00:42:28.780
of some of these programs
that are sort of hybrid

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:31.470
between vertical and horizontal programs.

00:42:31.470 --> 00:42:32.693
So do you think,

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:39.720
now is the time for a PEPFAR
level effort specifically,

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:43.570
so not necessarily PEPFAR,

00:42:43.570 --> 00:42:46.850
but a vaccine distribution
effort that is of that scope?

00:42:46.850 --> 00:42:51.020
So some of us called for
it like in March of 2020.

00:42:53.216 --> 00:42:57.463
So, yes, we have discussed
that there is this,

00:42:58.690 --> 00:42:59.960
the issues of a separate fund,

00:42:59.960 --> 00:43:02.580
but it's more long term, etc.

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:06.640
But more immediately, do we
need something of that magnitude

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:09.050
for this problem that we have right now

00:43:09.050 --> 00:43:11.443
where we need an end to end solution?

00:43:13.169 --> 00:43:14.700
- Yeah, no, thank you for that question.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:17.103
It's such an important one.

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:22.770
And, again, in the same
way that President Biden

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:26.950
has really expressed tremendous support

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:31.450
of the global HIV response,
we were so pleased,

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:33.710
and I hope many of you participated in

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:38.710
or have been tracking the
global summit on COVID-19

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:44.430
that occurred around the UN
General Assembly in the fall.

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:50.070
Where the President, really
tried to mobilize the world,

00:43:52.370 --> 00:43:57.370
to step up on vaccination
across the globe,

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:03.530
putting bold targets out there of 70%,

00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:07.410
trying to get the coverage
rate to 70% across countries

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:10.780
and what that could look
like and how we as a globe,

00:44:10.780 --> 00:44:14.360
not just the United
States but we as a globe

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:16.600
need to sort of collectively
move in this way.

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:21.600
So, and we've since had
some follow up ministerials

00:44:22.810 --> 00:44:24.673
to really try and urge,

00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:29.733
the vaccine delivery and administration.

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:36.610
The US has been donating,
hundreds of millions of vaccine,

00:44:39.230 --> 00:44:42.360
one of the concerns that we have,

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:47.360
and I'm just giving an example
from the African context is,

00:44:50.090 --> 00:44:54.280
there's a lot of supply,
actually, right now,

00:44:54.280 --> 00:44:55.550
in many of the countries.

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:57.734
One of the many concerns

00:44:57.734 --> 00:45:02.734
is much of what we face
here as well, hesitancy,

00:45:04.059 --> 00:45:09.059
actually getting vaccination
administered in a timely way.

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:13.330
We're seeing some waste
of some of the vaccines.

00:45:13.330 --> 00:45:17.623
So these are questions that
are also being considered.

00:45:19.631 --> 00:45:24.520
- So, look, you have a
particular strength as a leader,

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:26.650
because your own background

00:45:26.650 --> 00:45:30.270
includes a lot of work
in the field itself.

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:32.830
And I hope we sort of
nurtured some of those values

00:45:32.830 --> 00:45:37.830
and attitudes here at Yale, at
an early part in your career.

00:45:38.920 --> 00:45:40.760
But what do you think,

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:45.130
how has that informed
your leadership style

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:50.000
as you have sort of risen to
sort of leadership positions

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:52.370
within the program itself?

00:45:52.370 --> 00:45:53.610
And is there anything,

00:45:53.610 --> 00:45:56.730
any insight that you can
share with our students

00:45:56.730 --> 00:45:58.100
from that perspective?

00:45:58.100 --> 00:45:59.650
Someone from the field,

00:45:59.650 --> 00:46:03.140
someone who has gotten
their hands dirty that,

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:06.073
someone who hasn't been
dropped into an agency,

00:46:07.030 --> 00:46:09.710
as an appointee with
a lot of other assets,

00:46:09.710 --> 00:46:11.250
but not necessarily field experience,

00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:14.012
but you have direct field experience.

00:46:14.012 --> 00:46:15.423
Any insights?

00:46:16.780 --> 00:46:19.377
- Well, I so appreciate that question.

00:46:19.377 --> 00:46:24.183
And speaking directly
to the students at Yale.

00:46:26.440 --> 00:46:28.550
So, on a personal level,

00:46:28.550 --> 00:46:31.313
let me just tell you a
little bit about why,

00:46:32.300 --> 00:46:35.550
the number one why I chose Yale,

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:38.220
and why Yale and the approach,

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:41.500
the comprehensive approach
of the public health program,

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:44.210
the global health program in particular,

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:48.677
was so instrumental in
forming me in my career,

00:46:52.450 --> 00:46:54.030
in my service,

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:58.603
that I've since been
doing 20 plus years since.

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:02.440
What is extraordinary,

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:05.300
what was extraordinary
then 20 plus years ago,

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:09.210
when I was a student there,
as a Master's student,

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:11.400
and what drew me in,

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:14.960
and what I know is still
consistent at Yale,

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:19.500
is the comprehensive
multi sectoral approach

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:22.793
of the global health effort.

00:47:23.760 --> 00:47:26.520
When I first started it, it was brand new,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:31.520
it was a newer field, a
newer part of the program.

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:35.510
But what I loved about it was,

00:47:35.510 --> 00:47:38.770
how we at the time,

00:47:38.770 --> 00:47:43.430
had professors and opportunities
from the medical school,

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:45.880
from the business school, from the,

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:50.170
there was a multi-sectoral approach

00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:52.833
that was absolutely critical.

00:47:54.200 --> 00:47:59.200
One of the experiences that
really changed my world,

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:02.950
was what many, I don't know

00:48:02.950 --> 00:48:05.850
if this is still part of
the curriculum at Yale,

00:48:05.850 --> 00:48:09.221
but the summer between
first and second year

00:48:09.221 --> 00:48:11.630
of the master's program,

00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:15.100
when there was some sort
of internship type work,

00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:20.100
that's when I went to
UNICEF to start that,

00:48:20.429 --> 00:48:22.190
to do my internship.

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:27.180
And that turned into a love of this work,

00:48:27.180 --> 00:48:31.180
that then turned into going to the field,

00:48:31.180 --> 00:48:35.660
that then turned into time in countries.

00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:40.660
And that began my love for this work,

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:44.640
and for the service,

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:47.663
that I've done as a career public servant.

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:50.313
As you noted, I'm not a political,

00:48:51.228 --> 00:48:54.610
I have been in the government

00:48:54.610 --> 00:48:59.540
and experienced some of the real struggles

00:48:59.540 --> 00:49:03.560
and issues that people face,

00:49:03.560 --> 00:49:07.970
in communities, all around the globe,

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:12.970
and to be a part of something,
no matter how big or small,

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:16.533
something that is really making
an impact on their lives,

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:20.450
is the greatest honor of my lifetime.

00:49:20.450 --> 00:49:24.890
And so it's just, it really
did start with that approach

00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:28.880
that Yale instilled in me,

00:49:28.880 --> 00:49:33.430
of service, of experiencing the field,

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:37.620
and translating science and research

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:40.343
into real public health impact, so.

00:49:43.430 --> 00:49:44.720
Yeah.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:48.980
- So that's wonderful to hear,
and you'll be happy to know,

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:51.510
that the Yale Institute for Global Health

00:49:51.510 --> 00:49:53.630
has actually formalized

00:49:53.630 --> 00:49:58.630
a global health leadership
fellows program,

00:49:58.930 --> 00:50:02.210
that is open to any
trainee and student at Yale

00:50:02.210 --> 00:50:06.660
from undergrad to actual
postdocs research and clinical,

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:09.790
and everyone in between
including graduate students, etc,

00:50:09.790 --> 00:50:13.400
that has a formal pre-departure component,

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:18.400
then we place them in global
health agencies like UNICEF,

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:22.250
as you did that, save the
children and many others.

00:50:22.250 --> 00:50:26.250
And then we will expand to
ministries of health as well.

00:50:26.250 --> 00:50:28.230
This is in addition to
field placement program,

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:31.364
this has a specific focus
on leadership and service,

00:50:31.364 --> 00:50:33.060
particularly public service.

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:37.010
And so that program has expanded
and it's good to know that,

00:50:37.010 --> 00:50:38.850
these kinds of investments pay off,

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:41.880
so this particular program
is new and expanded,

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:44.490
but the earlier efforts have,

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:46.280
I think, I can say confidently,

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:49.400
has had a high return on investment.

00:50:49.400 --> 00:50:53.013
And so, in terms of, going back to,

00:50:54.320 --> 00:50:59.320
a little bit of focus on
partnerships that you mentioned,

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:08.090
specifically, thinking about
how faith-based organizations

00:51:09.590 --> 00:51:13.570
can be leveraged to help with HIV AIDS,

00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:16.010
stigma, prevention and care.

00:51:16.010 --> 00:51:18.630
And what are the new (muffled speaking)

00:51:18.630 --> 00:51:22.330
'cause I know that PEPFAR
has, from the beginning,

00:51:22.330 --> 00:51:25.270
collaborated with
faith-based organizations,

00:51:25.270 --> 00:51:29.010
but what are the new directions

00:51:29.010 --> 00:51:30.690
in that kind of partnerships?

00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:32.220
And what are some of the lessons?

00:51:32.220 --> 00:51:33.960
Because look, other sectors,

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:36.180
other entities will have to partner

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:39.040
with faith-based entities
and organizations,

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:42.070
from the grassroots to the global level,

00:51:42.070 --> 00:51:47.070
and some lessons from PEPFAR
would be very, sort of,

00:51:48.330 --> 00:51:50.853
would be very useful for
those sectors as well.

00:51:51.860 --> 00:51:56.860
- Yeah, I know, it's such
an important question,

00:51:56.990 --> 00:52:01.990
and an important sector that
is critical to the response.

00:52:02.650 --> 00:52:04.838
You know, I mentioned
earlier in my remarks

00:52:04.838 --> 00:52:09.040
that there's something extraordinary

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:14.040
about the HIV fight and that
it brings so much diversity,

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:19.460
so many sectors together.

00:52:19.460 --> 00:52:22.070
Everything from it, and
part of part of that,

00:52:22.070 --> 00:52:24.213
is the bipartisan support from Congress,

00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:29.403
just as many Republicans as
Democrats love this program.

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:35.657
We have the most
conservative religious groups

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:43.140
that have critical roles to play

00:52:44.148 --> 00:52:48.423
in the response, as the most liberal,

00:52:50.728 --> 00:52:54.450
NGOs or LGBTQI plus organizations,

00:52:55.590 --> 00:52:59.200
our advocacy groups are so broad.

00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:02.340
So, again, there's something beautiful,

00:53:02.340 --> 00:53:06.140
about the global HIV fight

00:53:06.140 --> 00:53:08.130
that has brought so many people together.

00:53:08.130 --> 00:53:11.573
Faith-based organizations,
as you noted, Saad,

00:53:12.440 --> 00:53:13.680
faith-based organizations

00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:17.920
have been critical to this
response from the very outset.

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:21.130
And I talked a little bit

00:53:21.130 --> 00:53:23.570
about local community organizations,

00:53:23.570 --> 00:53:25.427
and I include faith-based organizations

00:53:25.427 --> 00:53:29.093
in that broader subset.

00:53:30.140 --> 00:53:32.950
Faith-based groups have been
there in these countries

00:53:32.950 --> 00:53:34.810
before we were there.

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:39.810
They have access to people in
ways that we absolutely need,

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:45.890
to talk about some very sensitive issues

00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:47.320
like stigma and discrimination,

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:50.513
today, I mentioned, we were on,

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:53.840
with all of the country offices,

00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:55.980
and we were talking specifically about

00:53:55.980 --> 00:53:58.900
involving faith-based organizations

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:02.250
in COVID vaccination for
people living with HIV,

00:54:02.250 --> 00:54:04.240
because there's a real hesitancy

00:54:04.240 --> 00:54:08.280
among pregnant women living with HIV,

00:54:08.280 --> 00:54:12.010
and their hesitancy about COVID vaccine

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:17.450
and the impact that it
might have on their child.

00:54:17.450 --> 00:54:20.690
And so, the conversation was around,

00:54:20.690 --> 00:54:23.280
well, how do we involve
faith-based organizations

00:54:23.280 --> 00:54:28.280
that are trusted institutions
in many of these countries,

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:33.820
and that have influence
in so many different ways.

00:54:33.820 --> 00:54:36.820
So again, I think they have played,

00:54:36.820 --> 00:54:38.300
they continue to play,

00:54:38.300 --> 00:54:41.593
and they will always be
needed in the response.

00:54:42.770 --> 00:54:44.310
- Wonderful.

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:46.363
And the last question is,

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.850
many, and I think I know
who asked this question,

00:54:51.850 --> 00:54:54.200
from our audience,

00:54:54.200 --> 00:54:57.100
someone with a sort of a
significant student role.

00:54:57.100 --> 00:54:59.350
The question is, many in
our audience are students

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:02.010
that aspire to work in global health.

00:55:02.010 --> 00:55:03.790
What is your advice to these students,

00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:07.200
as they consider what to do after Yale?

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:09.460
I know you have alluded
to some of that stuff,

00:55:09.460 --> 00:55:12.143
but I think, we'd love
to end on that note.

00:55:13.520 --> 00:55:17.260
- So what to do after Yale?

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:18.590
Well, the good news is,

00:55:18.590 --> 00:55:22.270
is you're getting a really
incredible experience

00:55:22.270 --> 00:55:23.530
while you're at Yale.

00:55:23.530 --> 00:55:24.920
And I would encourage you

00:55:24.920 --> 00:55:27.180
to seize all of these opportunities,

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:28.653
including that new,

00:55:29.530 --> 00:55:32.300
or the fellowship that
you were describing Saad,

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:34.610
the new, the way you've institutionalized

00:55:34.610 --> 00:55:37.310
that field experience, because
that's really exciting.

00:55:39.080 --> 00:55:43.693
You know, I would encourage
you to be open and flexible.

00:55:46.610 --> 00:55:51.610
It's amazing how, oftentimes,
as I reflect back now,

00:55:52.880 --> 00:55:56.160
oftentimes, there's never a direct route

00:55:56.160 --> 00:56:00.810
to where you're headed, when
it comes to your career.

00:56:00.810 --> 00:56:05.810
But there are experiences that
if you're open to seizing,

00:56:08.763 --> 00:56:13.763
it could move you in a way that
really compels you to focus.

00:56:14.880 --> 00:56:16.960
And that's what happened for me,

00:56:16.960 --> 00:56:21.960
there was a incredible opportunity,

00:56:22.940 --> 00:56:25.740
it was a fellowship at CDC,

00:56:25.740 --> 00:56:29.480
that was specifically focused on,

00:56:29.480 --> 00:56:32.107
integrating services across the,

00:56:32.107 --> 00:56:33.480
and this was domestic,

00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:38.480
but across the education
and health sectors.

00:56:38.600 --> 00:56:40.400
And that intrigued me,

00:56:40.400 --> 00:56:42.310
because of a lot of the work

00:56:42.310 --> 00:56:44.220
that we were doing in
the global health space

00:56:44.220 --> 00:56:46.170
of bringing different sectors together,

00:56:46.170 --> 00:56:49.250
and that's one of the
things I learned at UNICEF

00:56:49.250 --> 00:56:51.220
when I did my internship there.

00:56:51.220 --> 00:56:53.570
And that intrigued me.

00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:56.390
And so I applied and got that fellowship,

00:56:56.390 --> 00:56:58.520
and that set me on a course,

00:56:58.520 --> 00:57:01.990
that in terms of
integrating across sectors

00:57:01.990 --> 00:57:06.900
and integrating services for adolescents,

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:09.670
and that has been evolved.

00:57:09.670 --> 00:57:11.890
And, again, I just,

00:57:11.890 --> 00:57:15.440
being flexible and focused on the mission

00:57:16.331 --> 00:57:21.331
and on service that you're
compelled by, and inspired by,

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:23.710
I think really helps move forward.

00:57:23.710 --> 00:57:27.803
But also reach out to
people that you know,

00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:31.710
that are in various places, as well,

00:57:31.710 --> 00:57:35.110
because we are always
looking for good people

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.040
to join our teams.

00:57:37.040 --> 00:57:38.000
- Wonderful.

00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:38.833
Thank you.

00:57:38.833 --> 00:57:42.593
And as you alluded to that, PEPFAR,

00:57:42.593 --> 00:57:44.840
and I think if I can say so,

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:46.640
PEPFAR reflects the best of America.

00:57:48.240 --> 00:57:53.240
Bipartisan, with empathy,
with adequate resources,

00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:57.830
bringing in good, solid
technical expertise,

00:57:57.830 --> 00:57:58.750
but at the end of the day,

00:57:58.750 --> 00:58:01.840
partnering with communities and countries,

00:58:01.840 --> 00:58:03.670
and working as true partners,

00:58:03.670 --> 00:58:06.030
rather than parachuting in solutions,

00:58:06.030 --> 00:58:07.580
and having long-term commitment.

00:58:07.580 --> 00:58:12.580
So I hope, PEPFAR remains that example,

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:15.690
but doesn't remain the isolated,

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:17.500
one of the, no, I wouldn't say rare,

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:19.530
but one of the few examples

00:58:19.530 --> 00:58:24.530
of this country making solid
investments in global health,

00:58:25.770 --> 00:58:29.040
that results in this win win situation.

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:32.670
And I'm sure it's been a
privilege for you as well I hope,

00:58:32.670 --> 00:58:37.590
that to lead this incredible
program in the recent past.

00:58:37.590 --> 00:58:39.300
So thanks for coming.

00:58:39.300 --> 00:58:41.500
And thanks for your words,

00:58:41.500 --> 00:58:44.960
and engaging us in this conversation.

00:58:44.960 --> 00:58:45.960
- Thank you so much.

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:47.870
And thank you for the opportunity.

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:52.480
And it really has been a
privilege of a lifetime,

00:58:52.480 --> 00:58:55.400
to be a part of something

00:58:56.244 --> 00:59:00.840
that has really been a
movement around the globe.

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:05.200
So I am just so grateful
to have been with you today

00:59:05.200 --> 00:59:09.440
and shared how some of
my roots there from Yale

00:59:09.440 --> 00:59:14.310
have helped contribute to who I am today

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:19.310
and the service that I've
been so privileged to provide

00:59:19.620 --> 00:59:23.070
on behalf of the American people.

00:59:23.070 --> 00:59:24.860
So thank you very much.

00:59:24.860 --> 00:59:26.040
- Yeah, thank you, thanks for coming,

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:29.720
and thank you for all of
you who joined us today.

00:59:29.720 --> 00:59:31.930
We look forward to seeing
you at the next event,

00:59:31.930 --> 00:59:36.930
of YIGH's conversations
series in early 2022.

00:59:37.180 --> 00:59:41.860
We have a pretty exciting
program brewing for you,

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:43.023
and happy holidays.

00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:46.043
- Thank you.

